 |---|---|---|---|---|
 | P-T-18 | the committed `pay_or_test_v2` pack is not rebuilt byte for byte on Python 3.10, one of the two interpreters CI runs. World 21 (`myopic_tests__loose__4`) records `rule_loss_usd.two_step_value` 0.17; rebuilt on 3.10 it is 0.16. Python 3.12 made `sum()` of floats compensated, the reference sums expected values with `sum()`, and a loss near 0.165 rounds either way. My first hypothesis was that the split option I had just added to the generator changed the world; the same rebuild on 3.13 from the same source matches. | the v2 pack tests run in a Python 3.10 venv before the pilot split was generated: 1 of 69 failed (`test_each_world_is_the_first_admitted_seed_of_its_block_reproduced_byte_for_byte[21]`) | none to any number: the field is a diagnostic rule's loss, rounded to cents; no admission decision moved. The pilot split was generated on 3.13 to match the committed pack, and the pack tests are run there | open. A fix makes the reference's sums independent of the interpreter (`math.fsum`) and regenerates under a new pack id; the committed pack is the one the confirmatory plan names, so it is not regenerated in place |
 | P-D-26 | a case cannot pin the module its headline measure comes from. The version 2 driver added `pay_or_test_v2_reference.py` to its plans as an implementation pin of kind `reference` (the fix P-T-17 asks for), and `resolve_run_plan` refused every plan: `unreferenced implementation pins: ['procurement_pay_or_test_v2_reference']`. The resolver admits only pins that a family verifier declares, and the procurement verifiers are declared in `environment.py`, which every sealed procurement plan hashes, so the decision-loss reference cannot become a pin without a new environment identity | the driver's own tests, before any run (9 of 61 failed with that message) | none; no cell had run | open. The version 2 model plans seal the reference by digest instead (`headline_reference`, and the `sources` block `verify` checks), which stops a changed reference from resuming or verifying a run root but is outside the kernel's receipts. The general fix is issue #289's first item: a headline that is a receipt leaf |
+
+## 2026-10-16 — historical pay-or-test headline audit
+
+| id | defect | detection | cost | disposition |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| P-T-17 | The v1 headline report reads an unsealed action projection through an unpinned decision-loss reference; receipt replay alone does not certify paper Table 5.3. This row carries forward the paper branch's 14 October finding. | Tracing the report back to the original receipts. | No original score or evidence is changed. | Resolved for the six reported v1 panels on 16 October: `pay_or_test_reanalysis` audited all 144 original receipts, derived every decision loss from sealed parsed actions under source/case pins, and reproduced the original means and seeded intervals. The public projection regenerates those scores; the original evidence remains untouched. Future receipt-leaf integration remains P-D-26 / issue 289. |
+| P-T-19 | The first historical reanalysis ordered worlds by the pack instead of the original report's summary-row order. All 96 per-world losses matched, but the same seeded finite bootstrap yielded slightly different intervals; the initial assumption was that pack order reproduced the report. | Comparing rebuilt Table 5.3 with the paper: Gemini [49.1, 101.5] instead of [48.7, 101.4], Luna [46.9, 102.2] instead of [46.7, 102.7]. | No mean, trajectory or original artifact changed; the diagnostic output is retained outside evidence. | Fixed by binding the original row order as analysis metadata, independently of the JSONL storage order; exact table reproduction is checked before release. |
 | P-D-27 | the version 2 pilot cannot size a confirmatory run inside the plan's 96-world cap, and its question is not reached by either subject. In 48 pilot plays (12 worlds, two seeds each) Gemini 3.8 Flash never quoted or sampled a newcomer and played exactly the pay-the-premium rule ($69.24 a world, the rule's own loss on these worlds); GPT-6 Luna touched a newcomer in 1 of 24 plays. The probe the plan counts happened in 0 of 12 worlds for both. So the P1 contrast measures whether a subject tests at all, not whether it plans past one test. The per-world SDs of the three contrasts at two plays are $54.1 (Gemini less the one-step rule), $177.2 (Luna less the rule) and $136.2 (Gemini less Luna), which need 326, 3,464 and 2,047 worlds at the $10 effect and the first Holm level; 96 worlds detect $18.1, $59.3 and $45.6. Luna's play-to-play variance is 99% one play: on `myopic_tests__loose__1` at seed 87001 it awarded 54 units, off the order step, the environment voided the award (margin -$632, worse than walking away), and at seed 87002 it awarded 50. The plan's orientation figures from version 1 (SD $13.0 to $17.5, 23 to 37 worlds) did not transfer, as the plan warned | the pilot report, by the plan's sizing rule (`pay_or_test_v2_report.py --panel pilot`) | $0.2064 of pilot spend; no confirmatory identity was created | open. Per the plan, no confirmatory run; the plan states the smallest effects 96 worlds detect. A next design needs subjects that test newcomers in the first place (a stronger subject or a brief that does not make paying the premium the obvious move) before the planning-past-one-test contrast can be read |
